    { math: '1 · Packets, not letters', note: 'Data is chopped into small packets, each labelled with destination. Different packets can take different routes and arrive out of order — the network never promised neatness.' },
    { math: '2 · IP — the addressing layer', note: 'Every device gets an IP address (like 142.250.4.100). Routers forward each packet hop by hop, reading only the destination — no single router knows the whole path.' },
    { math: '3 · TCP — the reliability layer', note: 'Packets get lost; TCP re-requests them, reorders them, and throttles speed when the network chokes. Your file arrives whole because TCP rebuilt it.' },
    { math: '4 · DNS — the phone book', note: 'Humans type names (google.com); the Domain Name System translates them to IPs, cached at every level for speed.' },
    { math: '5 · HTTPS — the trust layer', note: 'TLS encrypts the connection and proves the server is who it claims (certificates). The padlock icon is mathematics holding the middle of the internet shut.' },
  ],
};

const netQuiz = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: "A 10 MB file crosses a network that loses 1% of packets, with TCP retransmitting losses. Why does the file arrive intact?",
      choices: [
        "The network secretly uses better cables for large files",
        "TCP numbers every byte; the receiver detects gaps in the sequence and requests retransmission",
        "IP re-sends lost packets automatically",
        "Loss only affects small files",
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: "Reliability is assembled at the ends, not inside the network: sequence numbers expose any gap, ACKs drive retransmission, and reassembly restores order. IP itself is deliberately unreliable — 'best effort' only.",
    },
    {
      q: "Which physical fact sets a hard floor on the ping time from London to New York (about 5,600 km of fibre)?",
      choices: [
        "Router processing time — the dominant cost",
        "The speed of light in fibre (~200,000 km/s), giving roughly 28 ms one way",
        "TCP's congestion window",
        "DNS lookup time",
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: "Signals cannot beat light in glass — ~28 ms one-way, ~56 ms round trip before a single router acts. This is why CDNs exist: you cannot shorten physics, so you shorten distance.",
    },
    {
      q: "Your computer asks for example.com and the answer comes back in 2 ms. Where did it most likely come from?",
      choices: [
        "The 13 root servers",
        "The authoritative name server for example.com",
        "A local cache — your OS or resolver had a record still inside its TTL",
        "The web server itself",
      ],
      answer: 2,
      why: "The full DNS walk (root → .com TLD → example.com's nameserver) takes tens of milliseconds; the 2 ms answer was cached, and caches expire by each record's TTL — which is also why DNS changes take 'a while to propagate'.",
    },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/networking-how-the-internet-works">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Computer Science lesson, and it builds the internet from one
    organising idea rather than assuming it. From the foundational tier you need
    <em>Binary and bits</em> (what a packet actually carries) and <em>Growth of functions</em>
    (why repeated round trips dominate); from the intermediate tier, <em>Programming
    fundamentals</em> supplies the mental model of layered machinery. No networking knowledge
    is assumed — the ladder is built here, layer by layer.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain packet switching, and why the network promises only best effort;</li>
    <li>trace one page load through DNS, the TCP handshake, TLS and HTTP, with realistic timings;</li>
    <li>describe how TCP manufactures reliability and fairness without any central control;</li>
    <li>separate bandwidth from latency, and compute transfer times and physics floors;</li>
    <li>map digital currency and cyber security onto the same layer stack.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The internet is <strong>a packet-switched network with no owner and no centre</strong>, and its one
    organising idea is <strong>layering</strong>: each layer solves one problem and trusts the layers below —
    wires carry bits, IP routes packets between addresses, TCP makes delivery reliable, DNS makes addresses
    memorable, TLS makes them trustworthy. Designed under a 1960s military brief to survive partial failure
    (ARPANET, 1969; Cerf and Kahn's TCP/IP, 1974), it degrades gracefully instead of collapsing. This lesson
    traces one page load hop by hop, then attaches the syllabus's companion topics — the web proper, digital
    currency, and security — to the same machinery.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the journey of one page request, layer by layer" widget="equationStep" config={netCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the five cards and watch responsibility
    climb the stack: the network interior stays simple and best-effort throughout, while IP
    routes, TCP guarantees, DNS names and TLS secures. That deliberate simplicity let one
    1970s design absorb video, voice, gaming and apps nobody predicted.
  </p>

  <h2>The packet principle: why everything arrives in pieces</h2>
  <p>
    The internet's founding choice is <strong>packet switching</strong>: data is chopped into small packets
    (typically about 1,460 bytes of payload each) that share the wires statistically, instead of reserving a
    dedicated circuit like the telephone network. A 10 MB file becomes roughly 10,485,760 / 1,460 ≈
    <strong>7,180 packets</strong>, each labelled with source and destination addresses. The consequences are
    the internet's character: no circuit to hold idle, so capacity is shared efficiently among bursty users;
    no single path required, so packets can take different routes and arrive out of order, duplicated, or
    not at all — <strong>the network never promised neatness</strong>. All the neatness you experience is
    manufactured at the endpoints, which is the design's deepest lesson: <strong>best-effort inside,
    guarantees assembled at the edges</strong>.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a file sent over the internet travels as one unbroken
  stream down one wire, like water in a pipe." That is how the telephone network worked. In
  reality your file is chopped into thousands of
  labelled packets that share the wires with everyone else's and may take different routes,
  arriving out of order, duplicated, or not at all. The neatness you experience is
  manufactured at the endpoints by TCP: best effort inside, guarantees assembled at the
  edges.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> data crosses the internet as thousands of independent packets
    that share the wires statistically and arrive in any order. Everything from here tames
    that chaos.
  </p>

  <h2>IP: addressing and hop-by-hop routing</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Internet Protocol</strong> gives every machine an address and every packet a best-effort
    delivery attempt. IPv4 addresses are 32 bits — about 4.3 billion, which ran out (hence NAT boxes
    sharing one public address among a household, and IPv6's 128-bit addresses, enough for every device
    anyone can imagine). <strong>Routers</strong> forward each packet independently: read the destination,
    consult the forwarding table, send toward it — a hop at a time, with a TTL field decremented at every
    hop so misrouted packets die rather than loop forever. No router knows the whole path; the path is an
    emergent fact, which is why <code>traceroute</code> reveals a different route each run. Between
    organisations, routes are negotiated by <strong>BGP</strong> (the border gateway protocol) — essentially
    trusted announcements ("these prefixes are reachable through me") — a protocol old enough that its
    trust model (route announcements taken largely at face value) is a standing security story the next
    lesson picks up.
  </p>

  <h2>TCP: manufacturing reliability and fairness</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Transmission Control Protocol</strong> runs at both ends and turns lossy datagrams into an
    ordered, complete byte stream. Its mechanisms are worth knowing precisely because they are so reusable:
    <strong>sequence numbers</strong> label every byte, so the receiver detects any gap; <strong>ACKs</strong>
    confirm receipt; missing ranges are <strong>retransmitted</strong>; out-of-order arrivals are buffered
    and reassembled. On top sits <strong>congestion control</strong> — TCP's polite genius: each sender
    starts slowly (about 10 packets), roughly <strong>doubles its sending rate every round trip</strong>
    until packets drop, then cuts back sharply and probes upward again. The result is the famous
    <strong>sawtooth</strong> on throughput graphs, and something better than reliability: the internet's
    shared links are divided among competing flows automatically, by feedback rather than central control.
    Note what latency already costs: light in fibre travels about 200,000 km/s, so London–New York has a
    physical floor near 28 ms one way (~56 ms round trip) before any router or protocol acts. Bandwidth is
    buyable; latency is physics — the reason content delivery networks move data closer to you.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "my files always arrive intact, so the network hardware
  must be reliable." The opposite is the design: packets are lost constantly, and the
  reliability you experience is a protocol running on unreliable parts. TCP numbers every
  byte, detects the gaps, re-requests the missing ranges and throttles the sender when loss
  signals congestion. Remove TCP and the same cables deliver a garbled stream — the
  guarantee lives in software at the edges, not in the wires.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> IP routes packets hop by hop toward numeric addresses, and TCP
    reassembles them into an ordered byte stream at the far end. One problem remains: humans
    cannot remember the numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>DNS: the phone book and its caches</h2>
  <p>
    Humans type names; machines need numbers. The <strong>Domain Name System</strong> is a globally
    distributed, hierarchically cached database: your resolver asks a <strong>root server</strong> ("who
    handles .com?"), then the <strong>TLD server</strong> ("who handles example.com?"), then the domain's
    <strong>authoritative nameserver</strong> — and every answer is cached, with each record carrying a
    TTL that says how long it may be reused. The full walk happens rarely (tens of milliseconds, seconds
    only when cold); the common case is a cached answer in single-digit milliseconds. DNS's design made
    the internet scalable and human-usable at once — and its redirects are so trusted that DNS hijacking
    became a classic attack, again foreshadowing the security lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>The 200-millisecond assembly</h2>
  <p>
    Now the whole trace for one page load: <strong>1. DNS</strong> (~20 ms, usually cached) — example.com
    becomes an IP. <strong>2. TCP handshake</strong> (1 round trip) — SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK: "may I?" "yes,"
    "starting." <strong>3. TLS handshake</strong> (1–2 round trips, fewer with session reuse) — the server
    presents a certificate your browser checks against known authorities, and both sides derive session
    keys (the padlock: encryption plus identity). <strong>4. HTTP request/response</strong> — GET /, and
    the HTML arrives; the browser parses it and fires requests for every image, stylesheet and script it
    discovers, reusing the connection (HTTP/2 multiplexes them over one TCP stream). Every click repeats
    steps 2–4 — which is exactly why page-performance work is mostly about fewer round trips, smaller
    payloads and caching. Note the separation of concerns: <strong>the internet (IP) and the web (HTTP +
    HTML) are different layers</strong> — the web is one application riding the network, replaceable
    without touching the infrastructure below.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the full trace assembles DNS, TCP, TLS and HTTP into roughly 200
    milliseconds of coordinated work. Before attaching the syllabus's companion topics, the
    two performance axes deserve their own arithmetic.
  </p>

  <h2>The syllabus companions: digital currency and the security layer</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Digital currency</strong> is networking applied to trust. A cryptocurrency like Bitcoin is a
    <strong>distributed ledger</strong>: transactions are broadcast peer-to-peer (no bank in the middle),
    grouped into blocks, and each block is chained to the last by including its hash — so rewriting history
    means recomputing every block after it. <strong>Mining</strong> is a deliberately expensive lottery
    (proof of work) that decides who appends the next block, making the ledger expensive to rewrite; the
    famous 51% caveat is the system's honest boundary — a party controlling most of the network's hashing
    power could reorganise recent blocks. The design brilliance (trust from mathematics and incentives
    alone, fixed supply — Bitcoin's 21 million) and the real costs (enormous energy use, irreversible
    mistakes, volatility) are both facts about the same mechanism. <strong>Cyber security</strong> rides
    the same layers: encryption and authentication live in TLS, but the threats — interception, spoofing,
    denial of service, malicious payloads — attack specific layers, and defences must be matched to
    layers. The full threat-and-defence treatment is the dedicated lesson on this ladder; here, keep the
    mapping: DNS attacks poison the phone book, BGP hijacks steal routes, packet sniffing exploits
    unencrypted layers — layering is both the internet's strength and its attack surface map.
  </p>

  <h2>Bandwidth, latency, and the arithmetic of downloads</h2>
  <p>
    The two performance axes are independent, and their arithmetic is worth owning. <strong>Bandwidth</strong>
    is how much per second: a 1 GB file over a 100 Mbps link takes 1,000 MB × 8 / 100 ≈ 80 seconds — no
    protocol genius changes that; capacity is capacity. <strong>Latency</strong> is how long a bit takes
    to arrive, and it dominates interactive experience: the same 1 GB link with a 60 ms round trip
    delays every small exchange (each HTTPS handshake, each API call, each keystroke-echo) by that fixed
    tax, no matter how fat the pipe. Web performance is therefore mostly a latency war: fewer round
    trips (TLS session resumption, HTTP/2 multiplexing, preconnecting), shorter distances (CDN edge
    nodes putting the server 20 ms from the user instead of 200), and smaller payloads so fewer packets
    are needed. The physics floor from earlier still applies — light in fibre — and it explains the
    industry's shape: bandwidth got cheap (1000× in two decades) while round-trip time to the far side
    of Earth stayed ~150 ms because Earth's circumference is not negotiable. Any design that hides the
    round trip (caching at the edge, speculative prefetching, local-first data) is a design that has
    made peace with physics.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "upgrading to a faster connection makes everything feel
  snappier, since both are sold as speed." Bandwidth (how much per second) and latency (how
  long a bit takes) are independent axes. The 1 GB file does finish sooner on a fatter link,
  but every interactive exchange still pays the same fixed round-trip tax, and light in
  fibre sets a floor no purchase lifts. That is why page-performance work fights round trips
  and payload sizes rather than raw capacity.</p>

  <h2>The application zoo: mail, ports, and sessions</h2>
  <p>
    The web is the most visible application on TCP/IP, not the only one — and the zoo shares one
    addressing trick: <strong>ports</strong>, 16-bit numbers that multiplex many services over one IP
    address (80/443 web, 25/587 mail submission, 143 IMAP, 53 DNS). Email's anatomy shows the layering:
    <strong>SMTP</strong> moves messages between servers (a store-and-forward relay chain, visible in
    every Received: header), <strong>IMAP</strong> or POP fetch them to your device, and the mail is
    just structured text plus attachments carried by MIME — protocols composed, not fused. HTTP's own
    model deserves precision: it is <strong>stateless</strong> (each request stands alone), which is why
    state had to be rebuilt at the edges — <strong>cookies</strong> (small server-issued identifiers the
    browser replays, the basis of sessions and, famously, of tracking), tokens in headers for APIs, and
    cache headers (Cache-Control, ETags) telling proxies and browsers what may be reused. Status codes
    encode the contract (200 success, 301/302 redirects, 403 forbidden, 404 missing, 429 rate-limited,
    5xx server-fault) — and reading an outage as "4xx means our request was wrong, 5xx means their
    server is wrong" is genuinely useful debugging literacy.
  </p>

  <h2>Layering as the master idea, one more pass</h2>
  <p>
    It is worth restating the trace as a stack, because the stack is the field's most transferable
    abstraction. Physical (fibres, radio — moves bits); link (Ethernet, Wi-Fi — moves frames between
    directly connected devices); network (IP — moves packets across the whole internet, hop by hop);
    transport (TCP/UDP — moves bytes reliably, or fast and unreliably, between programs); application
    (HTTP, DNS, SMTP — moves meaning). Each layer's header is the envelope the layer below must deliver,
    and each layer may be swapped without rewriting the ones above (Wi-Fi to fibre mid-journey; TCP to
    QUIC; HTTP to whatever follows). The syllabus's three topics all live at identifiable floors: the
    web at application; digital currency as an application-layer trust protocol riding TCP/IP with no
    special hardware; cyber security as controls matched to floors — encryption at application and
    transport, firewalls at network, physical security below all. When something breaks, the debugging
    ritual walks the stack from the bottom (cable? Wi-Fi?) upward (DNS? TCP? the app?) — and the same
    discipline, "isolate the failing layer," is how distributed systems are debugged generally, which is
    why this unit's ideas reappear in the expert rung's distributed-systems lesson nearly unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In one sentence each: what do IP, TCP, DNS and TLS each contribute to a page load?</li>
    <li>A 2 GB file crosses a 200 Mbps link. Ignoring latency, roughly how long does the transfer take?</li>
    <li>Why is DNS built as a hierarchy of caches rather than one central phone book?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) IP routes packets to numeric addresses, best effort; TCP
    reassembles them reliably at the endpoints; DNS translates names to addresses and caches
    the answers; TLS encrypts and proves the server's identity. (2) 2,000 MB × 8 ÷ 200 ≈ 80
    seconds — the section's 1 GB example doubled on both axes. (3) One central server would
    be a single point of failure, a bottleneck and an attack target; caching at every level
    puts answers milliseconds away and lets lookups scale — the price is that changes
    propagate only as each record's TTL expires.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The internet is packet-switched and centreless: best effort inside, guarantees assembled at the endpoints.</li>
    <li>IP routes hop by hop; no router knows the whole path — the path is an emergent fact.</li>
    <li>TCP manufactures reliability and fairness by feedback: sequence numbers, ACKs, slow start, the sawtooth.</li>
    <li>DNS is a hierarchy of caches; each record's TTL decides how long an answer may be reused.</li>
    <li>Bandwidth and latency are independent; latency has a physics floor set by light in fibre.</li>
    <li>The web, digital currency and security all ride the same ladder — layering is both the strength and the attack surface map.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The natural next steps in this subject's advanced tier are <em>Cybersecurity: threats and
    defences</em>, which expands the attack-surface mapping sketched above, and <em>Web
    development: HTML, CSS and JavaScript</em>, which builds on the application layer you
    have just traced. <em>Operating systems basics</em> completes the picture of where the
    sockets actually run.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three ideas take you to the research frontier. <strong>The end-to-end principle</strong>: keep the
    network simple and put intelligence at the edges — the design choice that let the internet absorb
    video, voice, gaming and apps nobody predicted, and the argument underneath the net-neutrality debates
    (should the middle be allowed to favour some edges?). <strong>Layering under pressure</strong>: real
    deployments bend the model — NAT boxes violate IP's end-to-end addressability, middleboxes inspect
    traffic, and QUIC (HTTP/3) moves retransmission and encryption into UDP at the application layer
    specifically to escape TCP's head-of-line blocking and the OS's slow evolution — a reminder that layers
    are conventions, renegotiated when physics or business demands. <strong>Congestion at scale</strong>:
    classic loss-based algorithms mistake buffering for congestion; Google's BBR estimates actual
    bandwidth and round-trip time instead, and bufferbloat (oversized queues adding hundreds of
    milliseconds of latency) is an active engineering battleground. Open questions: can the routing core
    ever be made verifiable (SCION and similar redesigns say: partially), how will the internet carry
    exabyte-scale AI training traffic, and who governs a network that was designed to have no owner — the
    IETF's "rough consensus and running code" is the internet's constitution, and it is being tested.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cerf &amp; Kahn, "A Protocol for Packet Network Intercommunication" (1974); Kurose &amp; Ross, <em>Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach</em>; RFC 793 (TCP), RFC 1035 (DNS). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: reliability, physics and caches" widget="quiz" config={netQuiz} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each question's feedback names the layer doing the work
    — TCP's sequence numbers, the speed of light in fibre, the DNS cache and its TTL. If any
    answer surprised you, reread the matching section above: the quiz is a layer-by-layer map
    of this lesson, not a memory test.
  </p>
</TopicPage>
